# Code-shape rules backed by guards (Default)

These standing code-shape rules are each backed by a mechanical guard where possible, and by the code review's Standards axis where not.
A rule naming a guard lands in the same PR as its guard: never point at a guard not yet on `main`.
An allowlist or baseline file a rule uses is captured against the commit it was seeded from and only shrinks.
A PR that removes a violation lowers or removes its entry in the same PR, never leaving it slack.

**Each project picks its own tools.**
The guards are described by what they enforce, not by tool, since stacks differ.
At stage 0 (`.forge/feature-pipeline.md`) the project chooses its equivalents (a ruff- or eslint-style linter, a vulture- or knip-style dead-code finder, a coverage tool, a size measurement) and wires each into `.pre-commit-config.yaml` and the guard scripts under `scripts/`; until then, the shipped guard scripts are stubs that say so.

## Code comments say what the code can't

Write a comment only when deleting it would let a competent reader make a mistake.
Four kinds earn their place:

1. **Invariant**: what this code keeps true, led by the requirement or ADR ID when one is the reason.
2. **Guard reason**: why a check exists, or why it sits where it does.
3. **Hazard note**: a coupling to code the reader has no reason to open, such as "if you change this, also change X" or "must run before Y".
   This is the one place a little history belongs: the past failure, in a clause, as evidence for the hazard.
4. **Interface contract**: on a module, class or public function, what it promises callers that the signature does not say (units, ownership, error modes, concurrency).

Implementation comments say why, never what.
Restating the code, narrating history (which review found it, the chain of earlier fixes), design rationale beyond a line, and a TODO without a tracking issue are not comments: history belongs in the commit, PR and issue, where `git blame` reaches it, and design rationale in the design doc or ADR, linked by ID.

Default to one or two lines.
A comment earns more only when the hazard, guard reason or contract is genuinely multi-part (several call sites to touch, several failure modes, or a sequencing constraint that won't fit one clause), and even then stays a list of short clauses, one per part, not a narrative paragraph.
Length earned by restating the code, walking through how the change was made, or defending it is not this exception; that belongs in the commit, PR or ADR.

A docstring is a one-line summary, plus contract lines only when not obvious.
A module docstring names the module's responsibility in a few lines and links its design-doc section rather than restating it.
Do not rewrite comments in code your change does not touch, but tidy a history block into this shape when your change touches it.

An agent reads every comment in a file it edits, so narration costs context on every read, anchors the agent on behaviour the code no longer has, and gets copied into the next change.

## Module and function size cap

Keep a module under {{ max_module_lines }} lines and a function under {{ max_function_code_lines }} code lines (blank lines, comments and docstrings excluded; a nested function counts toward its parent), in every language in the repo.
A file or function already over a cap when the cap landed is tracked in a shrink-only allowlist; new code never gets a new entry.
Modules grown past what anyone can safely change in one sitting are expensive to split later.

The guard is `scripts/check_module_size.py` against `scripts/module_size_allowlist.json`, run as a pre-commit hook; each project points it at its own language's function boundaries at stage 0.
Both caps render from the `max_module_lines` and `max_function_code_lines` answers in `.copier-answers.yml`, so a cap changes by changing its answer, and changing it (for example `copier update --trust --defaults --skip-tasks --vcs-ref <_commit> --data max_function_code_lines=<n>`, with `_commit` from `.copier-answers.yml` so the TheForge version does not move) moves this rule and the guard together.
`--defaults` is required non-interactively, as for the coverage-floor command in `.forge/ci-and-checks.md`: without it Copier re-asks every other question and dies with `EOFError` with no TTY to answer them.
Vendored skill files under `.agents/skills/` are third-party code the project does not split, so the guard skips them.

## Adopt the abstraction early, then stay on it

For any generic concern, adopt a mature library rather than hand-rolling one: data access, provider calls, scheduling, retries, parsing, auth and caching are typical, but not the limit.
Decide at design time: the design doc names the library chosen and the alternatives rejected, and the architecture description lists every adopted abstraction.

Once adopted, code goes through it.
A bypass (raw SQL beside the ORM, a raw HTTP call beside the SDK client) is a deliberate decision, allowed when performance or functionality needs it, with a one-line comment at the site and an ADR when it spans more than one call site.
An unexplained bypass is a review finding.
Each concern lives in the module that owns it, and other code calls that module rather than the library.

The build-versus-buy record lives in the design doc's slice, a design-exit ticket's Structure guidance, or else the PR body.
When that record depended on `research`'s findings, it also gets an ADR (`.forge/feature-pipeline.md`, stage 5's build-versus-buy bullet), a second ADR case beside the multi-call-site bypass.
Before hand-rolling more than about 20 lines of generic infrastructure (process lifecycle, locking, retries, caching, validation, auth), find the library; fixing the same class of bug twice in one hand-rolled helper is the signal you should have.

The guard is review only (the Standards axis).
A mechanical import-boundary guard is added for a specific library only once a bypass of it has actually recurred in review, recorded in the ADR that adopted the library.

## A safety-critical process step needs a tested mechanism, not just reviewed prose

When a step in a forge-owned script or skill is safety- or security-critical (getting it wrong deletes the wrong lines, disables a guard, or ships an unreviewed change), build it as a scripted, tested function, not prose a human or agent follows by reading.
A post-hoc shape check on the result is no substitute: it catches some wrong outcomes, not a wrong procedure.
Give each such mechanism its own guard test, following this repository's own guard-test convention: it goes red when the check it verifies is removed or weakened.

A step whose core decision is a genuine judgement call (such as merging conflicting free-text content) may stay human- or agent-reviewed, but its mechanical part (deciding what is safe to handle automatically) is still scripted and tested, and the boundary between "handled automatically" and "flagged for review" has its own completeness test, so nothing falls through unflagged.

The guard is review only, via the Standards axis; a mechanical guard is added for a specific mechanism once a violation of it has actually recurred, the same convention as "Adopt the abstraction early, then stay on it" above.

## Dead code

A function, method, attribute, class or variable nothing reaches any more is removed, not kept in case it is needed later, and a comment or docstring describing behaviour the code no longer has is the same problem.
Findings already present when the guard lands are seeded into a baseline and fixed as separate tracked work.

The guard is `scripts/check_dead_code.py`, which runs the project's chosen dead-code finder and compares its findings with the shrink-only `scripts/dead_code_baseline.json`.
A count that drops without its entry being lowered fails like a new finding, and a finder exit code outside its own success codes fails, so a crashed finder never reads as clean.
Names a framework calls by convention (route handlers, hooks, entry points) go in the finder's configuration, not the baseline.

## No goldens or snapshots

Agents do not write golden-file or snapshot tests (`.agents/skills/tdd/tests.md`, "Golden and snapshot tests"): a regenerated golden asserts the implementation back to itself, and nobody has time to review that diff when it goes red.
When exact text really is the requirement (a user-facing message, a wire format another system parses), write it in the test as a short literal.

The guard is the `no-snapshot-assertions` pre-commit hook (`scripts/check_no_snapshots.py`), banning the common snapshot-assertion APIs; a project extends its pattern list for its own stack.

## Pinned dependencies (Never)

Every third-party dependency is consumed at a recorded exact version or digest, never a floating tag or range: direct and development libraries, container images (shipped base, CI service, devcontainer base), CI actions, devcontainer features, package-manager dependencies, pre-commit hook dependencies and vendored files.
Transitive dependencies are locked too, through the stack's lock file.
A version or digest move is its own ticketed PR, reviewed like code, never bundled into an unrelated change.
Language runtimes and the package manager's own self-upgrade are toolchain, held at a minor version rather than pinned exactly.

A rebuild from a release tag must get exactly what was tested, and a floating tag can change between builds with no record.
The guard is `scripts/check_pins.py`, which the `pinned-dependencies` pre-commit hook runs on every commit, because a commit that only deletes a lock file passes no file a trigger could match.
It ships as a stub saying it is not running, and stage 0 fills in checks that fail on any unpinned manifest line, missing lock file, image reference without a digest, or action reference without a full commit SHA.
